task: dataflow-source-log
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-11
branch: feature/dataflow-source-log
worktree: ../okay-wt-dsl1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 11

why: the repository's thesis is one primitive, the durable log, and
  the dataflow engine has never read from it. A worker DERIVES its
  partition from parameters, which was the honest choice for a
  benchmark and is not a source. Stage 11's first box is the log as
  the source, and it costs almost nothing because both halves already
  fit: `Streams` reads a topic partition in chunks, `Flow.Src` holds a
  thunk per partition.

what, in this session (the operator's budget is nearly out, so the
  stopping point is chosen rather than reached):

  BOX 1 ONLY. `Streams.chunks(topic, partition, from, chunk)` in
  okay-persist: a blocking, iterator-backed `Chunks[Record]` that
  ends when a read returns nothing — three lines, no new dependency in
  either direction, because okay-persist already sees okay's `Chunks`
  and okay-cluster's `Flow.of` takes any thunk. A test in okay-cluster
  (persist in test scope) fills a MemoryStore topic with the synthetic
  feed and asserts that a Job whose flow is the topic answers, in a
  batch run and in a stream, what the fan over the array answers.

  NOT in this session, and the design is written here so the next one
  starts rather than re-derives:

  BOX 2, seeking by epoch. Today a resumed session catches up by
  replaying epochs 1..N-1 and discarding. A seekable source should
  instead be OPENED at epoch N-1's end: offset = from + (N-1) x take
  when chunks are exact, which they are not (`take` is a floor over
  chunk boundaries), so the session must record the offset it reached
  per epoch — one Long beside `extent` in `Resp.Epoch` — and a fresh
  session asked for epoch N starts its `rest` at the journalled
  offset of N-1. `Flow.Src` thunks therefore take a start:
  `Vector[Long => Chunks[A]]`, with the array-backed sources ignoring
  it. That is the API change, and it is the whole of box 2.

  BOXES 3-4, the sink into a topic whose append is the commit, and
  exactly-once from log to log under a coordinator death, are stage
  9's `committed`/`recovered` over `Topic.append` with the epoch in
  the key — no design question left, only the code and the test.

predict: box 1 lands in one gate. The finding, if there is one, will
  be about `take`: an epoch of `take` ELEMENTS does not fall on a
  chunk boundary of the topic, and whoever writes box 2 must not
  assume it does.
